      "observed": "Eight condition combinations are described: SP/SA live/dead in nutrient/saline media. Live biomass varies 0.5–4 g wet/L, dead 1.5 g wet/L. Five repeated Ca analyses of the same sample are analytical replication, not five reactors. Sterile contamination controls and separate mixed-flow abiotic references are reported.",
      "needed": "Independent reactor/biological replicate identifiers, sample timing, matched biomass-free batch controls and correspondence to Table 3. Appendix 2 does not supply these metadata."
    },
    {
      "id": "W6",
      "status": "published_scope_limit_retained",
      "topic": "2009 extrapolation",
      "locator": "Berner (2010) DOI 10.2475/05.2010.04 p. 424; author reply DOI 10.2475/05.2010.05 pp. 425–426",
      "observed": "The comment challenges extrapolation from wollastonite to basalt and field biota; the reply agrees that wollastonite is a misleading general proxy and narrows the interpretation.",
      "needed": "No new clarification needed to retain this published scope limit. These pieces do not correct the assay loadings, units or rate table and are not retractions."
    },
    {
      "id": "D1",
      "status": "unresolved",
      "topic": "2021 nutrient MES pH",
      "locator": "Table 1 p. 3 D2-2/D2-3; Fig. 2A p. 3; SI Fig.S1A p. 1",
      "observed": "D2-2 pH 7.80 and D2-3 pH 8.05 for MES with 10% nutrient broth, versus Fig. 2A pH 6.1±0.1 and S1A D2 pH 6.1. Table 1 D2-1/D2-4 sterile stages are pH 6.17/6.05.",
      "needed": "Original pH readings, buffer/nutrient preparation, exact D2 stage labels and figure grouping. Neither table nor figure is preferred without clarification."
    },
    {
      "id": "D2",
      "status": "unresolved",
      "topic": "2021 endpoint factor",
      "locator": "Table 1 p. 3, paired D1–D4 stage1/stage4 rows; results p. 2; SI S1 p. 1",
      "observed": "Initial/final factors from printed Ca,Mg,Si log means: D1=1.51,2.88,1.62; D2=2.19,2.63,2.45; D3=0.72,0.72,1.32; D4=0.50,0.31,0.30. These are not a uniform 3–5 decline. D4 printed end rates exceed start rates.",
      "needed": "Original inlet/outlet concentrations, flow, mineral mass/surface area and selected steady-state samples/windows. These arithmetic diagnostics do not settle whether Table 1 or the prose/plots should change."
    },
    {
      "id": "D3",
      "status": "unresolved",
      "topic": "2021 time and steady state",
      "locator": "Methods p. 8, bacterial mixed-flow reactor",
      "observed": "25 mL reactor and 0.018–0.022 mL/min give five volume renewals in 3.95–4.82 days, consistent with one 4–5-day statement; a later statement says mechanical steady state after 24 h. Initial chemical equilibration is typically 2 weeks; subsequent cell stages 7–10 days; terminal sterile stage>3 weeks.",
      "needed": "Timestamped switch and sampling log, distinction between mechanical and chemical criteria, and actual per-run acceptance windows. Hydraulic arithmetic does not prove when chemical steady state was reached."
    },
    {
      "id": "D4",
      "status": "unresolved",
      "topic": "2021 drift and aging comparator",
      "locator": "SI Fig.S1A–D; main pp. 2,6,8",
      "observed": "S1 shows time-dependent pre-cell baseline decline and later changes through successive sterile, low-biomass, high-biomass and terminal sterile stages. The same mineral is used through a sequence. Main discussion cites earlier abiotic stability experiments.",
      "needed": "A simultaneous aging bacteria-free comparator for each condition, linked sample dates and run-level uncertainty. Earlier literature and successive stages do not remove aging as an alternative explanation for this sequence."
    },
    {
      "id": "D5",
      "status": "unresolved",
      "topic": "2021 microscopy control",
      "locator": "Results p. 4 versus Fig. 5a caption and embedded image p. 6; discussion pp. 6–7",
      "observed": "Results identify panel a as dead-fungus material; the caption says sterile electrolyte and image says no fungus. Results describe etching/rounded edges in live treatments while discussion broadly denies preferential dissolution sites.",
      "needed": "Original SEM specimen labels, control biomass status and image-to-reactor mapping. Attachment and qualified surface changes remain usable; a definitive causal control comparison or molecular passivation mechanism does not."
    },
    {
      "id": "D6",
      "status": "unresolved_for_exact_run_mapping",
      "topic": "2021 replication and fungal biomass",
      "locator": "Table 2/Fig.3 p. 4; Fig. 2 caption p. 3; methods p. 8; SI S2 p. 2",
      "observed": "Table 2 has six fungal summary rows: duplicate dead, live without nutrient and live with 1% nutrient groups. No dead-plus-nutrient arm. Figure 3 describes two reactors with 9–10 samples each over 19 days. S2 examples list 4.5/4.2/5.6 g wet/L, while Table 2 dead 4.46/4.95, live-no-nutrient 4.83/5.1 and live-nutrient 5.6/5.1. Figure 2 describes 2–4 duplicates with 3–4 steady-state samplings each; methods require at least four measurements.",
      "needed": "Replicate-level correspondence, actual biomass time series, exact sampling counts/criteria and SEM-to-reactor keys. Preserve published counts as stated without treating all timepoints as independent biological replicates."
    }
